ing; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Zion","term":"Zion"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Zion","sourceTitle":"Zion"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.1-5.15","r":"Mic.3.1-5.15","rawR":"Mic.3.1-5.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.1-5.15","value":"Mic.3.1-5.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1–5:15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This message indicts the evil leaders who were destroying God’s people and contrasts them with a glorious vision of God’s kingdom and the reign of a righteous king. After suffering judgment and exile, a purified people of Israel would return and experience God’s blessings."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.1-5.15","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.1-5.15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3,4,5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"09489c2234ae8c142dcf6abb929ad962acbe25e8bf4c5192ba9e73b6d58d556a","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.1-5.15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11270,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.1-5.15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.1-5.15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.1-4","r":"Mic.3.1-4","rawR":"Mic.3.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.1-4","value":"Mic.3.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1-4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Micah relentlessly indicted the ",{"c":["leaders of Israel"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," because they were responsible for the peoples’ well-being. The horrors here depict the terrors of a people under siege (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.28.53","r":"Deut.28.53","rawR":"Deut.28.53","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.28.53","value":"Deut.28.53"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 28:53"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.6.29","r":"2Kgs.6.29","rawR":"2Kgs.6.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.6.29","value":"2Kgs.6.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 6:29"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.1-4","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.1-4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e4eb3549c56ffce7b98583308f2d6bfa6b88a33e52892e28454958d2455c6963","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.1-4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11271,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.1-4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.1-4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.1","r":"Mic.3.1","rawR":"Mic.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.1","value":"Mic.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The leaders, who were ",{"c":["supposed to know"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," true judgment and justice (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.6.8","r":"Mic.6.8","rawR":"Mic.6.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.6.8","value":"Mic.6.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.10.12-13","r":"Deut.10.12-13","rawR":"Deut.10.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.10.12-13","value":"Deut.10.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 10:12-13"},"), were morally responsible for Israel’s guilt."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.1","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"4611f3c78b78e24eb66da8f9b7bb5e60c925eb54427bb66ae0ddcaaf4357db20","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11272,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.2-3","r":"Mic.3.2-3","rawR":"Mic.3.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.2-3","value":"Mic.3.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:2-3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The leaders, like wild animals, destroyed their own people."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.2-3","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.2-3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a22f991d74222abb9e7b981d853070771a36169b0ed140e38feb44e21c1f0b1c","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.2-3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11273,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.2-3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.2-3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.4","r":"Mic.3.4","rawR":"Mic.3.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.4","value":"Mic.3.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Then:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Even after oppressing the Lord’s people, the leaders would selfishly ",{"c":["beg"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," for ",{"c":["help"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," from ",{"c":["the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.4","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"464bb0660e99227b28500bccbf8293ec0c4dd4243d3512bddfcfdc1b5d7561cf","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11274,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.5","r":"Mic.3.5","rawR":"Mic.3.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.5","value":"Mic.3.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["false prophets"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," were among the spiritual leaders of Israel, so they fell under Micah’s accusations. Prophets were supposed to call Israel to the true way, not to send them astray (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.13.1-18","r":"Deut.13.1-18","rawR":"Deut.13.1-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.13.1-18","value":"Deut.13.1-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 13"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.18.1-22","r":"Deut.18.1-22","rawR":"Deut.18.1-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.18.1-22","value":"Deut.18.1-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"18"},"). These prophets used their gifts to benefit themselves."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.5","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d2e828d8785e08792297e9a9a8bf35763902c1ceb48502f0cebc87ddbc3930bc","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11275,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.6-7","r":"Mic.3.6-7","rawR":"Mic.3.6-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.6-7","value":"Mic.3.6-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:6-7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Micah announced God’s judgment on the false prophets. Without God’s special communications, these seers and fortune-tellers were like the pagan court prophets of such nations as Babylon, Mari, and especially Assyria, who were expected to toe the party line but who had no real revelation from the Lord (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.28.6","r":"1Sam.28.6","rawR":"1Sam.28.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.28.6","value":"1Sam.28.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 28:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Amos.8.11-12","r":"Amos.8.11-12","rawR":"Amos.8.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Amos.8.11-12","value":"Amos.8.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Amos 8:11-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.6-7","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.6-7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ba381ef1d99da4e47ea87e53f38c0adba45ef38bee77f3d82dc0fd9157ab2256","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.6-7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11276,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.6-7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.6-7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.8","r":"Mic.3.8","rawR":"Mic.3.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.8","value":"Mic.3.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," There was a strong contrast between the true prophet and the false prophets (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.5-7","r":"Mic.3.5-7","rawR":"Mic.3.5-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.5-7","value":"Mic.3.5-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:5-7"},"). Truth, ",{"c":["justice"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["power"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," come from God’s ",{"c":["Spirit"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," who gave Micah the moral and ethical ",{"c":["strength"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to declare his true message about the ",{"c":["sin and rebellion"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of his people."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.8","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1fd4dfefbf77daaacdc3b732da9fdd36d23ad728c324b7622343af0ca700d598","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11277,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.9-12","r":"Mic.3.9-12","rawR":"Mic.3.9-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.9-12","value":"Mic.3.9-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:9-12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["leaders of Israel"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," were ",{"c":["building Jerusalem"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," on a ",{"c":["foundation of murder and corruption"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". Because of this, the city would be dismantled; it would be reduced to wilderness and ruins (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.12","r":"Mic.3.12","rawR":"Mic.3.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.12","value":"Mic.3.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:12"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.9-12","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.9-12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"693b4876abf0c85f7778b1746ef592f5d6fd33eb06123b5d1bfbea8b05ece0ce","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.9-12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11278,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.9-12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.9-12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.11","r":"Mic.3.11","rawR":"Mic.3.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.11","value":"Mic.3.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"},{"c":[" is here among us:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This expression indicates the Lord’s approval (see ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.3.12","r":"Exod.3.12","rawR":"Exod.3.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.3.12","value":"Exod.3.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 3:12"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.3.14","r":"Exod.3.14","rawR":"Exod.3.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.3.14","value":"Exod.3.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.25.8","r":"Exod.25.8","rawR":"Exod.25.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.25.8","value":"Exod.25.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"25:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.5","r":"Mic.2.5","rawR":"Mic.2.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.5","value":"Mic.2.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mic 2:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.2.11","r":"Zech.2.11","rawR":"Zech.2.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.2.11","value":"Zech.2.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Zech 2:11"},") and his dwelling among his people. The false prophets wrongly claimed God’s presence."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.11","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[3],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"457e4494df51a0db495b92e88bf00ec314ddc568cbfb9455bbca5e9fed67804a","sourceItemName":"Mic.3.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11279,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.3.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.3.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.3.12","r":"Mic.3.12","rawR":"Mic.3.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.3.12","value":"Mic.3.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Mount Zion"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," where the Lord once lived, would become ",{"c":["a thicket"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," an uninhabitable wilderness. Utter destruction awaited the fallen ",{"c":["Jerusalem"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". Jeremiah later quoted this passage (",{"href":"?bref=Jer.26.18","r":"Jer.26.18","rawR":"Jer.26.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.26.18","value":"Jer.26.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 26:18"},"). • Just as a ",{"c":["field"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," needs to be cleared to prepare it for cultivation, ",{"c":["Jerusalem"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," had to be ",{"c":["reduced to ruins"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in judgment."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.3.12","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":3,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":3,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.3.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","ty